|   `-- provider-sync\
|-- infra\
|   `-- compose.yaml
|-- docs\
`-- Start-LifeFlow.ps1
```

Start as a modular monolith. Do not introduce many independent microservices until load, team ownership or deployment requirements justify them.

## 6. PostgreSQL and PostGIS design

### 6.1 Proposed database schemas

| Schema | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `geo` | OSM places, boundaries, addresses and imported geometry |
| `catalog` | Canonical places, categories, media and opening hours |
| `housing` | Properties, units, listings, offers, amenities and availability |
| `mobility` | Transport providers, service areas, quotes and trips |
| `identity` | Users, preferences, saved places and consent |
| `workflow` | Plans, steps, execution state and approvals |
| `integration` | External providers, source identifiers and synchronization state |
| `audit` | Tool calls, data changes, approvals and security events |

### 6.2 Canonical place model

The central `catalog.places` record should include at least:

```text
id
name
category_id
geom
address
phone
website
status
verified
attributes
created_at
updated_at
deleted_at
```

Related tables should include:

- `catalog.place_categories`
- `catalog.place_sources`
- `catalog.place_media`
- `catalog.place_opening_hours`
- `catalog.place_revisions`
- `geo.import_runs`
- `geo.source_records`

Recommended spatial storage:

- `geometry(Point, 4326)` for map output and spatial relationships
- Optional `geography(Point, 4326)` for meter-based distance calculations
- GiST indexes on spatial columns
- B-tree indexes on source IDs, categories and workflow foreign keys
- Trigram or full-text indexes for place and address search

### 6.3 Source and precedence model

Every record must retain its origin:

```text
source=osm
source=partner
source=user
source=lifeflow_admin
```

Default precedence:

```text
Verified LifeFlow data
  > verified partner data
  > approved user data
  > imported OSM data
```

An OSM update must not overwrite verified LifeFlow fields or delete private places. Missing imported records should first be marked stale or inactive, not immediately hard-deleted.

### 6.4 Media storage

PostgreSQL stores media metadata and URLs. Image binaries should be stored in S3, MinIO or an equivalent object store.

## 7. Housing query and data contracts

### 7.1 Housing query scope

The objective of this subsystem is:

> Given explicit user constraints, return all suitable housing or listing candidates that satisfy those constraints, together with structured factual attributes needed by downstream services.

This subsystem owns:

- Housing data contracts
- PostgreSQL/PostGIS housing data model
- Housing data ingestion and source traceability
- Exact numeric, categorical, text and spatial filtering
- Nearby-place constraints
- Route and commute constraints through OSRM
- Housing query API and read-only MCP tools
- Query correctness and determinism tests

This subsystem does not decide:

- Which listing is best
- Which listing a user should choose
- Personalized ranking
- Recommendation scoring
- LLM-based recommendation or recommendation explanations

Recommendation is owned by a separate MCP or module and consumes the factual contracts produced here.

### 7.2 Terminology and constraint ownership

```text
search     = retrieve and filter factual data
compare    = place factual attributes side by side
recommend  = apply preferences, ranking or advice
```

This plan is responsible for **search**. A factual comparison may be added later, but it must not declare a winner, score a listing or provide personalized advice.

Hard constraints are mandatory. A returned record must satisfy every applicable hard constraint. For example:

```text
district = District 7
bedrooms >= 2
price_amount <= 15000000
price_period = month
area_m2 >= 60
hospital_distance_m <= 3000
```

Soft preferences may be parsed and preserved in query metadata. The housing query subsystem must not convert a phrase such as `near a park would be nice` into a score unless a deterministic rule is explicitly defined by a versioned contract.

### 7.3 Housing entity separation

The data model must distinguish the physical location from changing commercial records:

```text
catalog.places
       |
housing.properties
       |
housing.units
       |
housing.listings
       |
housing.offers
```

| Entity | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `catalog.places` | Canonical geographic place and address |
| `housing.properties` | Long-lived building, house or property |
| `housing.units` | A specific apartment, room or rentable unit |
| `housing.listings` | A source-specific publication advertising a unit/property |
| `housing.offers` | Time-varying price, deposit, availability and commercial terms |

An early dataset may not populate every entity fully, but contracts and identifiers must not collapse property, unit, listing and current offer into one ambiguous record.

### 7.4 Base housing record

The housing contract must be machine-readable and shared by the API, MCP server, agent, frontend and external Recommendation MCP.

Minimum factual fields:

```text
property_id
unit_id
listing_id
offer_id
place_id
title
property_type
listing_type
price_amount
currency
price_period
deposit_amount
bedrooms
bathrooms
area_value
area_unit
address
location
amenities
availability
images
source
source_url
verified_at
extra_attributes
```

Validation rules:

- Currency and pricing period are explicit.
- Area units are explicit; the preferred normalized unit is square meters.
- Missing values are represented as `null`; no service or agent may invent them.
- Invalid values produce structured validation errors.
- `extra_attributes` preserves supplied information not yet modeled.
- Valid and invalid example records accompany the schema.
- Contract versions are explicit and backward compatibility is documented.

### 7.5 `HousingSearchRequest` contract

The machine-readable request must support:

| Constraint | Fields |
|---|---|
| Text | `text` |
| Property | `property_type`, `listing_type` |
| Administrative area | `district`, `administrative_area_ids` |
| Bounding box | `bounding_box` |
| Radius | `center`, `radius_m` |
| Price | `min_price`, `max_price`, `currency`, `price_period` |
| Rooms | `min_bedrooms`, `max_bedrooms`, `min_bathrooms` |
| Area | `min_area_m2`, `max_area_m2` |
| Amenities | `amenities` |
| Availability | `availability_from`, `availability_to` |
| Trust | `verification_requirement`, `source_filters` |
| Nearby | `nearby` place constraints |
| Commute | `commute` route constraint |
| Result control | `pagination`, `sort` |
| Unscored metadata | `soft_preferences` |

Conceptual valid request:

```json
{
  "administrative_area_ids": ["district_7"],
  "listing_type": "rent",
  "max_price": {
    "amount": 15000000,
    "currency": "VND",
    "period": "month"
  },
  "min_bedrooms": 2,
  "min_area_m2": 60,
  "nearby": [
    {
      "category": "hospital",
      "max_distance_m": 3000
    }
  ],
  "commute": {
    "destination": {
      "lng": 106.7009,
      "lat": 10.7769
    },
    "max_duration_seconds": 1800,
    "profile": "driving"
  },
  "pagination": {
    "limit": 50,
    "cursor": null
  },
  "sort": [
    { "field": "price_amount", "direction": "asc" },
    { "field": "listing_id", "direction": "asc" }
  ]
}
```

Unknown values do not satisfy hard constraints by default. For example, a listing with `bedrooms = null` must not pass `min_bedrooms = 2`. Any future exception must be explicit in the request contract and visible in the response.

### 7.6 `HousingSearchResponse` contract

The response contains factual data only:

```json
{
  "query_id": "...",
  "contract_version": "...",
  "results": [
    {
      "listing_id": "...",
      "property_id": "...",
      "unit_id": "...",
      "offer_id": "...",
      "place_id": "...",
      "title": "...",
      "location": {
        "lng": 106.7,
        "lat": 10.77
      },
      "price": {
        "amount": 14000000,
        "currency": "VND",
        "period": "month"
      },
      "bedrooms": 2,
      "bathrooms": 2,
      "area": {
        "value": 78,
        "unit": "m2"
      },
      "amenities": [],
      "verification": {},
      "source": {},
      "matched_constraints": [],
      "unknown_fields": [],
      "nearby": [],
      "commute": null
    }
  ],
  "page": {
    "next_cursor": null,
    "returned_count": 1
  }
}
```

The response must not contain `recommendation_score`, `recommended`, `best_choice`, `best_listing` or LLM reasoning.

Allowed ordering is factual and deterministic, for example:

- Price ascending or descending
- Distance ascending
- Area ascending or descending
- Creation or verification time
- A stable ID tie-breaker

This ordering is search result ordering, not recommendation.

### 7.7 PostgreSQL/PostGIS query capability

`search_housing` must execute precise filtering in PostgreSQL/PostGIS rather than asking an LLM to filter records. It must support:

- Numeric filters for price, bedrooms, bathrooms and area
- Categorical filters for property type, listing type, amenities, source and verification
- Text search over normalized titles and addresses
- Administrative-area containment
- Bounding-box queries
- Center-and-radius queries
- `ST_DWithin` or an equivalent indexed nearby query
- Spatial indexes
- Cursor-based pagination
- Deterministic ordering with a stable tie-breaker
- Route/commute verification when requested

Examples such as `price <= 15000000`, `bedrooms >= 2` and `within District 7` are database constraints, not LLM judgments.

### 7.8 Nearby-place filtering

Nearby constraints use canonical places stored in PostGIS, not the frontend GeoJSON:

```text
housing candidate
       |
       | ST_DWithin / indexed spatial join
       v
catalog.places
category = hospital
```

For each matched nearby place, return factual fields such as:

```text
place_id
name
category
distance_meters
verification
source
```

If `max_distance_m = 3000` is a hard constraint, a housing result must have at least one valid matching place within 3,000 meters. Missing nearby data does not count as a match.

### 7.9 Commute filtering

Straight-line distance must not be used to claim that a commute is under a requested duration. Use this bounded pipeline:

```text
PostGIS hard filters
  -> coarse spatial prefilter
  -> enforce configurable candidate limit
  -> OSRM route checks for remaining candidates
  -> duration_seconds hard filter
  -> factual route data in response
```

For `max_duration_seconds = 1800`, only candidates with a successful OSRM route whose duration is at most 1,800 seconds may pass. Route failures, `NoRoute` and timeouts must be reported or excluded according to the explicit unknown/error policy; they must not be treated as satisfying the constraint.

The service must cap and batch candidate evaluation where supported. It must not issue uncontrolled OSRM requests for thousands of records.

## 8. MCP server design

The MCP server exposes controlled capabilities to the LifeFlow Agent. Use schemas for input validation and structured results.

### 8.1 Read-only tools for the first release

```text
search_places
get_place
find_nearby_places
calculate_route
search_housing
get_housing
```

An optional future `evaluate_housing_constraints` tool may report whether specified candidates satisfy a versioned set of factual constraints. It must not rank, score or recommend candidates.

`compare_housing` is outside the first release. If introduced later, it may only return a factual table such as price, area, bedrooms, nearby distance and commute duration. It must not return a winner, personalized score or recommendation.

### 8.2 Recommendation MCP boundary

Recommendation is a downstream consumer owned by another team member or module:

```text
Housing Query API / MCP
          |
          | HousingSearchRequest
          | HousingSearchResponse
          | canonical IDs and factual route/nearby data
          v
External Recommendation MCP
```

The external Recommendation MCP may consume:

- `HousingSearchRequest`
- `HousingSearchResponse`
- Canonical property, unit, listing, offer and place IDs
- Factual nearby-place results
- Factual route and commute results

It must consume shared contracts, API responses or MCP tool results. It must not depend directly on internal housing tables. The Recommendation MCP is not implemented in this repository slice.

### 8.3 Write tools for later releases

```text
save_place
save_housing_shortlist
create_move_plan
request_transport_quote
submit_for_approval
confirm_booking
cancel_booking
```

These tools belong to the broader LifeFlow platform and are outside the current Housing Query implementation scope.

### 8.4 MCP resources

Suggested read-only resources:

```text
lifeflow://schemas/place
lifeflow://schemas/housing
lifeflow://catalog/categories
lifeflow://providers/capabilities
lifeflow://workflows/moving
```

User-specific resources must require authentication and authorization.

### 8.5 Tool safety

- No `execute_sql` or arbitrary HTTP tools
- Separate read tools from state-changing tools
- Validate every input and output schema
- Require `user_id`, authorization context and tenant context
- Require an `idempotency_key` for writes
- Require an approval token for bookings and payments
- Record calls, duration, result status and actor in `audit.tool_calls`
- Redact secrets and sensitive personal data from logs

### 8.6 Transport

- Use `stdio` for local development and MCP Inspector testing.
- Use authenticated Streamable HTTP for the LifeFlow application environment.
- Bind local development servers to `127.0.0.1` unless remote access is explicitly required.
- Pin the MCP SDK version and record protocol compatibility.

## 9. End-to-end agent flow

Example request:

```text
Find a two-bedroom apartment in District 7 below 15 million VND,
within 3 km of a hospital,
and with a commute under 30 minutes.
```

Expected execution:

```text
1. Parse a structured HousingSearchRequest.
2. search_housing queries candidate listings.
3. Apply price, bedroom, area and location hard filters in PostgreSQL.
4. Use PostGIS to verify the hospital distance constraint.
5. Use OSRM to verify the commute-duration constraint.
6. Return only candidates satisfying all known hard constraints.
7. Include source, verification, matched constraints and unknown fields.
8. Frontend or the external Recommendation MCP consumes the factual results.
```

The Housing Query subsystem stops here. It does not rank candidates, recommend a listing or choose a winner. The map, API, MCP and external consumer must use the same canonical IDs.

## 10. Map integration plan

### 10.1 Current state

```text
MapLibre -> public/data/accommodations.geojson
```

### 10.2 Transitional state

```text
MapLibre -> GET /places?bbox=...&categories=...
                 |
                 v
              PostGIS

Fallback -> existing accommodations.geojson
```

### 10.3 Target state

The frontend requests only records visible in the current map bounding box. The API supports category, text, verification, source and distance filters.

Suggested API surface:

```text
GET  /places
GET  /places/{id}
GET  /housing
GET  /housing/{id}
POST /routes
POST /workflows
GET  /workflows/{id}
POST /workflows/{id}/approvals
```

## 11. OSM update pipeline

Never overwrite the currently working dataset in place.

```text
New versioned Vietnam PBF
  |-- Validate file and checksum
  |-- Clip HCM, Hanoi and Da Nang
  |-- Build OSRM in new versioned directories
  |-- Test new graphs on temporary ports
  |-- Import POI into database staging tables
  |-- Normalize categories and deduplicate
  |-- Compare counts and validation reports
  |-- Publish database changes transactionally
  |-- Switch routing containers after acceptance
  `-- Keep the previous version for rollback
```

Recommended naming:

```text
Raw\vietnam-YYYYMMDD.osm.pbf
Routing-HCM\versions\YYYYMMDD\
Routing-Hanoi\versions\YYYYMMDD\
Routing-Danang\versions\YYYYMMDD\
```

OSM and LifeFlow custom records must be merged by source-aware rules, not by replacing entire tables.

## 12. Custom basemap plan

Custom basemap work begins only after PostGIS, API and MCP read tools are stable.

```text
OSM PBF
  -> Planetiler
  -> PMTiles or compatible vector tiles
  -> MapLibre style.json
  -> Maputnik
  -> LifeFlow visual style
```

The basemap should contain roads, water, parks, buildings and administrative labels. Hotels, rentals, hospitals, partners and recommendations remain dynamic business layers.

Desired LifeFlow style:

- Clean and minimal
- Clear primary roads
- Subtle secondary roads and buildings
- Light water and park treatment
- Clear city and district labels
- Configurable category icons and colors
- No default POI overload

## 13. Security and operational requirements

- Separate database roles for migrations, ingestion, API and read-only jobs
- No application superuser credentials
- Authentication and tenant authorization on API and MCP requests
- Encryption for secrets and sensitive personal data
- Approval workflow for financial or booking actions
- Audit logs for tool calls and state changes
- Database backups and tested restoration procedures
- Health checks for PostgreSQL, MCP, API and OSRM
- Metrics for latency, failures, route errors and tool usage
- Rate limiting for public or external endpoints
- Timeouts and circuit breakers for partner APIs

## 14. Implementation phases

### Phase 0: Preserve the baseline

- Inventory current files, versions, ports and container configuration
- Record checksums for source PBF files
- Export PostgreSQL schema and create a verified backup
- Keep the existing frontend and GeoJSON fallback working
- Preserve current browser acceptance tests

Exit criteria: Existing map, POI and routing behavior is reproducible and recoverable.

### Phase 1: Shared contracts

- Define place, housing and route schemas
- Define `HousingSearchRequest` and `HousingSearchResponse`
- Add valid and invalid examples
- Define null, unit, currency and additional metadata behavior
- Version contracts and document compatibility

Exit criteria: API, MCP, agent, frontend and external Recommendation MCP can consume the same factual schemas without inventing fields or depending on internal tables.

### Phase 2: PostGIS foundation

- Enable PostGIS without replacing the existing database
- Add versioned migrations
- Create schemas, roles, constraints and indexes
- Add ingestion and audit tables

Exit criteria: Spatial queries work with indexes and all migrations are reversible or have documented recovery steps.

### Phase 3: POI and housing ingestion

- Import current accommodation data into staging
- Normalize, deduplicate and publish canonical places
- Preserve all source properties and IDs
- Compare source, staging and published counts
- Implement the property, unit, listing and offer separation required by available housing data
- Record missing fields, validation failures and source provenance

Exit criteria: All 13,120 source POIs are accounted for, including rejected or duplicate records, and available housing records can be queried without collapsing long-lived properties and time-varying offers.

### Phase 4: Platform API

- Implement `search_housing` and `get_housing`
- Add numeric, categorical, text, bounding-box and radius filtering
- Add nearby-place filtering through canonical PostGIS places
- Add route adapter for the three OSRM endpoints
- Add authentication and structured error responses

Exit criteria: API integration tests prove hard-filter correctness, deterministic pagination, missing-value behavior, nearby constraints, OSRM commute constraints, offline errors and no-route responses.

### Phase 5: Read-only MCP

- Implement MCP server using shared services and contracts
- Add `search_housing` and `get_housing`
- Add `search_places` and `find_nearby_places`
- Add `calculate_route`
- Test with MCP Inspector
- Add audit logging and input/output validation

Exit criteria: The agent can search housing, find nearby places and calculate routes without direct database access.

### Phase 6: Frontend migration

- Load visible POI from the Platform API
- Retain current GeoJSON as temporary fallback
- Use canonical place IDs in map, chat and workflows
- Preserve current city switching and routing UX

Exit criteria: Existing browser tests pass and the map no longer needs to load all POIs at startup.

Recommendation implementation is explicitly out of scope for Phases 0-6. A separately owned Recommendation MCP may consume the published contracts and factual results after the Housing Query foundation passes its acceptance tests.

### Phase 7: Workflow and approval tools

- Create moving-plan workflow
- Add quotes, approvals and execution states
- Add idempotent write tools
- Integrate transport providers behind adapters

Exit criteria: No booking, payment or external commitment can occur without explicit approval and an audit record.

### Phase 8: Custom basemap

- Build versioned PMTiles
- Create and validate a LifeFlow style
- Host tiles with rollback support
- Keep business data outside the basemap

Exit criteria: The map can run with the LifeFlow basemap while POI and routing behavior remains unchanged.

### Phase 9: Production hardening

- Backups, monitoring and alerts
- Security review and load testing
- Scheduled OSM/provider updates
- Disaster recovery and rollback exercises

Exit criteria: Recovery, update and operational procedures are documented and tested.

## 15. Acceptance criteria for the complete platform foundation

- Existing MapLibre and OSRM functions do not regress.
- Existing source files and graphs remain untouched.
- Current POI data is fully traceable from source to database.
- Custom records survive an OSM refresh.
- Nearby and bounding-box queries use spatial indexes.
- MCP tools return structured and validated results.
- The agent has no arbitrary SQL capability.
- The same place ID works across map, API, MCP and workflow records.
- Financial and booking actions require explicit approval.
- Every write operation is idempotent and auditable.
- Database and routing updates support rollback.
- Custom basemap styling does not contain private business data.

### 15.1 Housing Query acceptance criteria

| Case | Given | Required result |
|---|---|---|
| Price | `max_price = 15,000,000 VND/month` | No result exceeds 15,000,000 VND/month |
| Bedrooms | `min_bedrooms = 2` | No result has fewer than two bedrooms |
| Missing bedroom value | `bedrooms = null` and `min_bedrooms = 2` | The record does not satisfy the constraint |
| Area | `min_area_m2 = 60` | Every result has a known normalized area of at least 60 m2 |
| Radius | `radius_m = 5000` | Every result is inside the indexed spatial query radius |
| Nearby hospital | `max_distance_m = 3000` | Every result has at least one valid hospital within 3,000 meters |
| Commute | `max_duration_seconds = 1800` | Every result has a successful OSRM route of at most 1,800 seconds |
| Source traceability | Any returned listing/place | IDs and factual fields can be traced to source records |
| Unknown field | A source field is missing | The response reports `null`/unknown and does not invent a value |
| Determinism | Same database state and request | The same hard-filter result set and stable ordering are returned |
| Recommendation separation | Any housing search | No recommendation flag, score, winner or advice is returned |

If a hard constraint cannot be evaluated because required source data is unknown, the candidate does not pass by default. The response or query diagnostics should make this exclusion observable where appropriate.

### 15.2 Housing Query contract tests

Valid request example:

```json
{
  "administrative_area_ids": ["district_7"],
  "listing_type": "rent",
  "max_price": {
    "amount": 15000000,
    "currency": "VND",
    "period": "month"
  },
  "min_bedrooms": 2,
  "min_area_m2": 60,
  "nearby": [
    {
      "category": "hospital",
      "max_distance_m": 3000
    }
  ],
  "commute": {
    "destination": {
      "lng": 106.7009,
      "lat": 10.7769
    },
    "max_duration_seconds": 1800,
    "profile": "driving"
  }
}
```

Required invalid cases:

- Negative price
- Unsupported currency
- Price amount without a pricing period where the contract requires one
- Longitude outside `[-180, 180]`
- Latitude outside `[-90, 90]`
- Negative or zero radius where a positive radius is required
- Negative bedroom or bathroom count
- Unsupported area unit
- Malformed or inverted bounding box
- Unsupported commute profile
- Pagination limit outside the configured range

## 16. Explicitly out of scope for the first implementation

- Housing recommendation
- LLM-based ranking or recommendation explanations
- Recommendation scores or personalized winner selection
- Implementation of the separately owned Recommendation MCP
- Payments and automatic booking
- Production deployment
- Public remote MCP access
- Live traffic estimation
- Satellite imagery and street view
- Public transport routing
- Custom PMTiles basemap
- Replacing the current OSRM graphs
- Removing the GeoJSON fallback
- Migrating every LifeFlow service into separate microservices

## 17. Decisions requiring approval

- [x] Use a modular monolith for the initial platform
- [x] Use TypeScript for the Platform API and MCP server
- [x] Use PostgreSQL with PostGIS as the system of record
- [x] Store media metadata in PostgreSQL; object storage is deferred until media ingestion
- [x] Start with read-only MCP tools
- [x] Use `stdio` locally; authenticated Streamable HTTP remains deployment work
- [x] Keep OSRM and the current MapLibre UX unchanged during Phases 1-5
- [x] Use versioned imports and preserve rollback inputs
- [x] Require approval for every future booking, payment or external commitment
- [x] Defer the custom basemap until the data and tool foundation is stable
- [x] Limit this repository slice to factual Housing Query behavior
- [x] Treat the Recommendation MCP as an external consumer of shared contracts

## 18. Recommended first implementation slice

After this plan is approved, implement only:

1. Shared Place contract
2. Shared Housing contract
3. `HousingSearchRequest` and `HousingSearchResponse`
4. Route contract
5. PostgreSQL/PostGIS migrations and restricted roles
6. Housing and POI ingestion with source traceability
7. `search_housing` Platform API
8. `get_housing` Platform API
9. Nearby-place query through PostGIS
10. OSRM route adapter and bounded commute filtering
11. MCP tools: `search_housing`, `get_housing`, `find_nearby_places`, `calculate_route`
12. Contract, query and integration tests

Do not implement `recommend_housing`, LLM ranking, recommendation scores, bookings, payments or custom basemap work in this slice.

